 First, we can simplify the expression by finding a common denominator. We have
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x} + \frac{\cos x}{1 + \sin x} &= \frac{\sin x(1 + \sin x)}{\cos x(1 + \sin x)} + \frac{\cos x \cdot \cos x}{\cos x(1 + \sin x)} \\
&= \frac{\sin x + \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x}{\cos x(1 + \sin x)}.
\end{align*}
Using the Pythagorean identity \(\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1\), we can simplify further:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin x + \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x}{\cos x(1 + \sin x)} &= \frac{\sin x + 1}{\cos x(1 + \sin x)} \\
&= \frac{1}{\cos x} \\
&= \sec x.
\end{align*}

<answer> Thus, the simplified expression is <answer>sec x</answer>

